# {ID} — {Title} (REQ)

> Owns WHAT and WHY, not HOW. Written as a use case (actor + system), not
> free prose — this is the format `/tn-new-req` and `confirm_import.py`
> both scaffold into, so every REQ in the graph reads the same way and an
> agent can load it directly as a spec without re-interpreting prose.

## Actor(s)

Who initiates this? Name a role, not a person — e.g. "Cashier",
"Scheduled job", "Support agent". If more than one actor, list each with
what they're responsible for.

## Goal

One sentence: what does the actor want to accomplish, in their own
terms — not "implement X module".

## Preconditions

What must already be true about the system before this use case can
start?

## Main flow

Numbered actor/system exchange — this is the backbone `TEST-UAT-*`
verifies against:

1. Actor: ...
   System: ...
2. Actor: ...
   System: ...

## Alternate / exception flows

- If <condition during step N>, then <system behavior> instead of
  continuing the main flow.

## Postconditions

What must be true when this use case succeeds?

## Out of scope

What this REQ explicitly does NOT cover — say it here instead of letting
scope creep into a sibling REQ or into the paired DESIGN.

## Acceptance criteria (feeds TEST-UAT-*)

- [ ] ...
- [ ] ...

